\details{
\subsection{Estimands}{

The outcome is modelled by one mean per occupied cell of the pair's 2x2
table (cells \code{00}, \code{10}, \code{01}, \code{11}, x then y), on the log-odds scale for a
binary outcome and the outcome's own scale for a continuous one. Every
reported quantity is a contrast of those means: \code{interaction} is
\code{mu11 - mu10 - mu01 + mu00}, and \code{a_vs_b} is \code{mu_a - mu_b}. A contrast is
estimable when every cell it uses is occupied, so which contrasts exist for
a pair depends on its occupancy, not on a fitted model.

Each pair is classified from its four cell counts alone, and the class
decides what is tested: the interaction for a fully occupied pair; for a
nested pair (one off-diagonal cell empty) the conditional contrast on the
occupied side; \verb{11_vs_00} for \code{identical} features; nothing for \code{disjoint}
(no double positives) or \code{degenerate} pairs, which are still reported.
\code{near_nested} pairs are tested on the interaction and on the conditional
contrast that avoids the thin cell; \code{no_double_negative} pairs on both
conditional contrasts. Because the choice never looks at the outcome, it
costs no multiple-testing budget.
}

\subsection{Testing}{

Only \code{tested} rows have p-values: a 1-df likelihood ratio test for a binary
outcome, the exact F test for a continuous one, each against the cell-means
model with the contrast set to zero. Both are two-sided, so a negative
interaction is found as readily as a positive one. Wald tests are not used:
they lose power badly as a logistic fit approaches separation. Adjusted
p-values are computed once over all tested rows of all pairs.

The tested family mixes hypotheses, so \code{p_adj <= 0.05} says that a \emph{pair} is
associated with the outcome, not that it has an interaction:
\code{occupancy_class} and \code{contrast} say which claim a row supports. A
\code{near_nested} pair's two tested rows are strongly correlated and may both be
significant; \code{any_significant} counts the pair once.
}

\subsection{Separation}{

When an occupied cell's samples are all cases or all controls, a logistic
estimate diverges. This is decided from the counts, which is exact here,
not from how close \code{glm()} gets to a fitted probability of 0 or 1. A
contrast that depends on such a cell is reported as \code{beta = Inf} or \code{-Inf}
with \code{se = Inf} when its direction is determined (\code{unbounded_positive},
\code{unbounded_negative}), and as \code{NA} when separated cells pull it both ways
(\code{indeterminate}). An empty predictor cell is a different matter: contrasts
using it are \code{not_identified}. A separated row keeps its p-value and stays
in the family: separation damages the estimate, not the likelihood ratio,
so significant rows with an infinite \code{beta} are expected. \code{n_eff} stays
finite on such rows; it measures the predictor cells, which are intact.
}

\subsection{Intervals}{

\code{ci_lo} and \code{ci_hi} bound a 95\% confidence interval that agrees with the
test: it excludes zero exactly when the row's p-value is below 0.05. For a
binary outcome it is a profile-likelihood interval, from a refit in which
the contrast is a single coefficient; for a continuous outcome the exact t
interval. Wald intervals (\verb{beta +/- 1.96 * se}) are not used. Rows without
a finite estimate have no interval, and nor, for now, do separated rows:
profiling a diverging coefficient gives meaningless bounds.

A profile interval costs several times a pair's fit and test, which is why
only tested rows get one by default. \code{ci = "all"} computes one for every
estimable row, up to seven per pair; \code{"none"} skips them.
}

\subsection{Continuous outcomes}{

The linear model pools one residual standard deviation over the four
cells, so the F test and every standard error assume the cells are equally
variable. Coreact's cells are typically unbalanced, with few
double-positive samples: if a small cell is more variable than the large
ones, contrasts using it get standard errors that are too small and
p-values that are too small. A continuous outcome taking only two values
draws a warning, since a binary outcome passed as continuous fits a linear
probability model without complaint. If every occupied cell of a pair is
constant, its F test returns its limit: \code{p = 0} when the tested contrast is
non-zero, \code{p = 1} when it is zero.
}

\subsection{Within a library}{

For a result of \code{run_coreact(data_x)}, which feature of a pair is \code{x} and
which is \code{y} follows index order and means nothing else. The directional
labels — \code{nested_y_in_x} against \code{nested_x_in_y}, \verb{11_vs_10} against
\verb{11_vs_01}, the sign of \verb{10_vs_01} — then describe orientation only.
}

\subsection{Provenance}{

\code{res} must carry the \code{provenance} attribute that \code{\link[=run_coreact]{run_coreact()}} attaches,
which the package uses to check that \code{data_x} and \code{data_y} are the objects
\code{res} was computed on and are given in the right order: by name, size,
number of positive cells and an MD5 hash of their contents. You need not use it
yourself. It is restored by \code{\link[=read_coreact_results]{read_coreact_results()}}, but lost when results
are read with \code{read_tsv()}, summarised or passed through base \code{merge()}.

Samples with a missing outcome are dropped before anything is counted, so
every count refers to the analysis sample (\code{n_used}). Dropping samples can
leave a pair below the prevalence or intersection threshold it was selected
under; a message says how many.
}
